<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1834 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Kde domov můj</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of the Czech Republic, first sung in a comedy at a Prague theatre in 1834.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Josef Kajetán Tyl</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>František Škroup</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Czechs; Czechoslovakia; the Czech Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Czech</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Prague, Czech Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first performed 21 December 1834; the Czech part of the anthem of Czechoslovakia from 1918; anthem of the Czech Republic from 1993</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Josef Kajetán Tyl, music by František Škroup, first performed 1834)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kde-domov-muj">Kde domov můj</a>&quot; began as one of twenty-one songs of incidental music that František Škroup composed for Josef Kajetán Tyl's comedy Fidlovačka aneb Žádný hněv a žádná rvačka (Fiddlers' Feast, or No Anger and No Brawl). Karel Strakatý first sang it at the Estates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 21 December 1834. The original song has two stanzas. Tyl is said to have thought of leaving it out of the play, unsure of its quality, but it soon became the most popular song of Fiddlers' Feast among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> who were seeking to revive their identity within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, and quickly became their unofficial patriotic anthem.</p><p>Composers and poets took it up. Johann Strauss the Younger worked its motif into his Slaven-Potpourri (Slavic Potpourri) of folk songs and dances, Op. 39, in 1847. In 1882 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonin-dvorak">Antonín Dvořák</a> used it in his overture to František Ferdinand Šamberk's play Josef Kajetán Tyl, often performed on its own as Domov můj (My Home), a concert work of some ten minutes. Rainer Maria Rilke paid tribute to the song in two German poems published in Larenopfer (1895).</p><p>Soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was formed in 1918, the first stanza of the song became the Czech part of the national anthem, followed by the first stanza of the Slovak song &quot;Nad Tatrou sa blýska&quot;, with official translations into German and Hungarian. The German-controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> (1939–1945) kept the song as its anthem, usually played together with the German anthems, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a> and the Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a>. When Czechoslovakia split in December 1992, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> kept &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kde-domov-muj">Kde domov můj</a>&quot; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> &quot;Nad Tatrou sa blýska&quot;; Slovakia added a second verse to its anthem, while the Czech Republic adopted its own in the single-stanza version. The words are set down in Appendix 6 of Czech Act No. 3/1993 Coll., as adapted by Act No. 154/1998 Coll.</p><p>The orchestration by Otakar Jeremiáš, in three versions (for large orchestra, 1930; for small orchestra, 1932; and a pastoral or Christmas version for organ and orchestra, 1932), is still performed. In 2008 the Czech government released new official recordings, orchestrated by Jeremiáš and Jaroslav Krček and performed by the National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> Opera Chorus and Orchestra under Jiří Bělohlávek in four versions: orchestral, choral, for soprano (Kateřina Kněžíková) and for bass-baritone (Adam Plachetka). They were first presented on Czech Statehood Day, 28 September 2008, in the Pantheon of the National Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The singer asks where his home is and answers with a gentle picture of the Bohemian countryside, its streams, meadows, woods and blossoming orchards, a land like paradise; the second stanza praises the Czech people who live there. The mood is quiet and lyrical rather than martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Czech</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Kde domov můj?!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Hučí voda po lučinách,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Bory šumí po skalinách,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">V sadě skví se jara květ,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Zemský ráj to na pohled!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">A to jest ta krásná země -</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Česká země - - domov můj!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Kde domov můj?!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Znáte v kraji bohumilém</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Tiché duše v těle čilém,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Jasnou mysl, znik a zdar,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">A tu sílu, vzdoru zmar;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">To je Čechů slavné plémě -</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Mezi Čechy - - domov můj!</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where is my home?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where is my home?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Waters thro' its meads are streaming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mounts with rustling woods are teeming.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Vales are bright with flowrets rare,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh, earth's Eden, thou are fair!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Land of beauty, dear Bohemia,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou art my home, my fatherland!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou art my home, my fatherland!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where is my home?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where is my home?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the Towers of God 'tis bounded;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the noblest sons surrounded;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>True and light of heart are they,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Firm and bold in deadly fray,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Offspring grand of dear Bohemia,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou art my home, my fatherland!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou art my home, my fatherland!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Josef Kajetán Tyl, Fidlovačka (1834): the song as sung by the fiddler Mareš. Text from <a href="https://cs.wikisource.org/wiki/Fidlova%C4%8Dka%2C_neb%3A_%C5%BD%C3%A1dn%C3%BD_hn%C4%9Bv_a_%C5%BE%C3%A1dn%C3%A1_rva%C4%8Dka%2FObraz_%C4%8Dtvrt%C3%BD" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: uncredited translation from the Chicago University Settlement Song Book, in Charities, vol. 13 (1904). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Charities%2FVolume_13%2FNumber_10%2FThe_Bohemian_National_Hymn%2C_My_Fatherland" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
